<Legal title="Privacy policy" description="What data huckepack processes when you visit, and your rights." updated="27 October 2026">
  <h2>In short</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No cookies, no tracking, no analytics, no ads, no accounts and no forms.</li>
    <li>Our host processes your IP address to deliver the pages, as every web server must.</li>
    <li>When a trail page shows its map, your browser loads map tiles from OpenFreeMap.</li>
    <li>Your light or dark mode choice is stored in your own browser only.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Controller</h2>
  <p>The controller under the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) is:</p>
  <address>
    <mark class="todo">First name Last name</mark><br />
    <mark class="todo">Street and number</mark><br />
    <mark class="todo">Postcode City</mark><br />
    Germany<br />
    Email: <mark class="todo">contact address</mark>
  </address>

  <h2>Hosting: GitHub Pages</h2>
  <p>
    This site is hosted on GitHub Pages, a service of GitHub, Inc., 88 Colin P. Kelly Jr. Street, San Francisco, CA
    94107, USA. When you open a page, your browser sends GitHub the data every web request carries: your IP address,
    the date and time, the page requested, the referring page, and your browser and operating system. GitHub uses
    these data to deliver the site and keep it secure, and may store them in server logs. We have no access to these
    logs and don't receive any visitor data from GitHub.
  </p>
  <p>
    Legal basis: Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR. Our legitimate interest is a site that is delivered reliably and securely.
    GitHub may process the data in the USA. GitHub is certified under the EU–U.S. Data Privacy Framework, on which the
    European Commission has based an adequacy decision (Art. 45 GDPR). Details:
    <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub
    Privacy Statement</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Maps: OpenFreeMap</h2>
  <p>
    Trail pages show a map with a basemap from OpenFreeMap (<a href="https://openfreemap.org/">openfreemap.org</a>). The
    map loads only when you scroll to it. Your browser then requests map tiles, fonts and the map style from
    <code>tiles.openfreemap.org</code> and sends it your IP address and browser details, as with any web request. We
    don't send OpenFreeMap anything else, and no cookies are set.
  </p>
  <p>
    Legal basis: Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR. Our legitimate interest is showing the trail on a map without paying for or
    building our own map server. If you'd rather not load the map, don't scroll to it; everything else on the page
    works without it.
  </p>

  <h2>Your browser's local storage</h2>
  <p>
    If you choose light or dark mode in the header, your browser remembers the choice under the key
    <code>theme</code> in its local storage. It never leaves your device, and we can't read it. Choosing "automatic"
    deletes it, and so does clearing your browser's site data. Storing it is strictly necessary to provide the setting
    you asked for, so it needs no consent (§ 25 (2) no. 2 TDDDG).
  </p>

  <h2>Links to other websites</h2>
  <p>
    Pages link to trail operators, data sources and licences. No data is sent to those sites until you follow a link;
    from then on, their privacy policies apply.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>Regarding personal data about you, you have the right to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>access it (Art. 15 GDPR),</li>
    <li>have it corrected (Art. 16) or erased (Art. 17),</li>
    <li>restrict its processing (Art. 18),</li>
    <li>receive it in a portable format (Art. 20),</li>
    <li>
      object at any time, on grounds relating to your particular situation, to processing based on Art. 6 (1) (f)
      (Art. 21).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    To use these rights, write to the address above. You may also complain to a data protection supervisory authority
    (Art. 77 GDPR), for example the one in the German state where you live or where we are based.
  </p>
  <p>
    We don't use automated decision-making or profiling. We only change this policy when the site changes how it
    handles data; the date at the top shows the latest version.
  </p>
</Legal>
